rs/QbieShay/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/QbieShay/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/QbieShay/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in Godot v4.7.dev3\n- Not Reproducible in Godot v4.7.dev2, Godot v4.6.2\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.7.dev3 - SteamOS holo on X11 - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 2 monitors - Vulkan (Forward+) - integrated AMD Custom GPU 0932 (RADV VANGOGH) - AMD Custom APU 0932 (8 threads) - 14.46 GiB memory\n\n### Issue description\n\nAfter enabling trails on a GPUParticle2D, the node's transform is applied twice to the emission position (e.g. the node at (100,100) will emit at (200,200)). Possibly a regression caused by https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/112447?\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. In a new project, create a GPUParticle2D with any texture and a ParticleProcessMaterial\n2. Enable Trails\n3. Drag the GPUParticle2D around in the editor to see that the emission point is incorrect\n(Note that the doubled emission transform continues to persist even if you disable trails after enabling it)\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nN/A","closed_by":{"login":"QbieShay","id":7917475,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc5MTc0NzU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7917475?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/QbieShay","html_url":"https://github.com/QbieShay","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/QbieShay/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/QbieShay/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/QbieShay/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/user